            <ArticleTitle>Increase of nitric oxide production by L-arginine potentiates i.c.v. administered beta-endorphin-induced antinociception in the mouse.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pretreatment (i.c.v.) of mice with L-arginine but not D-arginine potentiated beta-endorphin-induced (i.c.v. administered) inhibition of the tail-flick response. The potentiation was attenuated by N omega-nitro-L-arginine methyl ester, a selective inhibitor of nitric oxide synthase. This observation suggests that increased production of nitric oxide from L-arginine mediates the potentiation of beta-endorphin-induced antinociception.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
